page guide_host {
  title = "Host configuration and WSL 2"
  chapter_header "Host configuration and WSL 2" {
    kicker       = "The guide · explanation"
    reading_time = "9 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-31"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<'DOC'
Almost everything vmlab needs is in the lab file. What is left is per-host:
where it may allocate subnets from, which VNC viewer to launch, how big a
chunk to push to a registry, where the config-weave binaries are, how large a
file the workspace syncer carries, which UEFI firmware a VM boots. Those live in one optional host configuration
file. This chapter explains that file, the directories vmlab uses, what changes
when the host is WSL 2, and how vmlab picks between KVM and TCG. Every field is
in [Host configuration file](ref_host) and every path in
[Files and directories](ref_paths).
DOC

  h2 "The host config file"

  p <<'DOC'
vmlab reads `~/.config/vmlab/config.wcl`, honouring `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`. The
file is optional and every field in it is an override, so an absent file
means all defaults. It must start with `import <vmlab-host.wcl>` and contain
one `host { … }` block. A malformed value is reported at its line with the
same wording a lab file gets, and every mistake in the file is reported in
one pass. The errors surface where a lab is loaded, so a broken file fails
`vmlab up` with its lines named; the daemon processes themselves fall back to
defaults for a file they cannot read.
DOC

  code wcl { filename = "~/.config/vmlab/config.wcl" source = <<'SRC'
import <vmlab-host.wcl>

host {
  subnet_pool        = "10.99.0.0/16"
  dns_suffix         = "lab.local"
  disk_low_percent   = 5
  viewer             = "remote-viewer vnc://{}"
  oci_chunk_size     = 128MiB
  workspace_max_file = 2GiB
}
SRC
  }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "What it sets" | "Default" |
      | "`subnet_pool`" | "The CIDR segments with no `subnet` are auto-allocated from, one /24 each. See [Networking](guide_networking)." | "`10.213.0.0/16`" |
      | "`dns_suffix`" | "The suffix under which every machine's name is registered in segment DNS." | "`vmlab.internal`" |
      | "`dns_upstream`" | "The resolver `ip[:port]` segment DNS forwards to." | "the host's resolver" |
      | "`disk_low_percent`" | "The free-space percentage below which the `host.disk_low` watchdog fires. See [Events and handlers](guide_events)." | "`10`" |
      | "`psk`" | "The pre-shared key that authenticates cross-host segment trunks." | "none" |
      | "`trunk_port`" | "The TCP port the supervisor listens on for inbound cross-host trunks." | "`13947`" |
      | "`viewer`" | "The VNC viewer command; `{}` is replaced by the target." | "auto-detected" |
      | "`fastpath`" | "The network fast path: `auto`, `off`, `sockmap` or `afxdp`. See [vmlab fastpath](cmd_fastpath)." | "`auto`" |
      | "`oci_chunk_size`" | "The layer chunk size a template push uses. See [Distributing templates over registries](guide_registries)." | "`512MiB`" |
      | "`config_weave_bin_dir`" | "The directory holding the config-weave guest binaries. See [Playbooks](guide_playbooks)." | "`~/.local/share/config-weave/bin`" |
      | "`workspace_max_file`" | "The workspace syncer's per-file size guard; a larger file is refused by name." | "`256MiB`" |
      | "`firmware_dir`" | "A UEFI firmware override, laid out `<dir>/<arch>/`. See the UEFI firmware section below." | "vmlab's bundled firmware" |
      | "`btrfs_nocow`" | "Whether a machine's disk directory on btrfs gets NOCOW before its first disk. See Disks on btrfs below." | "`true`" |
  }

  p <<'DOC'
`config_weave_bin_dir` is a location knob with one code path behind it
rather than a switch: it is the first rung of a three-rung
lookup, ahead of the `VMLAB_CONFIG_WEAVE_DIR` environment variable and the
XDG default. `workspace_max_file` is host config rather than a `@dev`
argument because the cap is about your link to your guest, not about the lab
everyone shares, and the refusal message names it so "raise the cap" points
somewhere.
DOC

  h3 "Host GPUs"

  p <<'DOC'
A lab's `gpu {}` never carries a PCI address: which GPU sits at which address
is a fact about one machine, and the lab file travels. Each machine's host
config names its own GPUs instead, beside `host {}`, and marks one the
default. A lab names a host GPU with `host_gpu`, or takes the default. The
same lab then runs on two machines with different cards:
DOC

  code wcl { filename = "~/.config/vmlab/config.wcl (this machine)" source = <<'SRC'
import <vmlab-host.wcl>

gpu "AMD1"    { address = "0000:72:00.0" }
gpu "Nvidia1" { address = "0000:01:00.0"  default = true }
gpu "Nvidia2" { address = "0000:05:00.0" }
SRC
  }

  code wcl { filename = "~/.config/vmlab/config.wcl (a second machine)" source = <<'SRC'
import <vmlab-host.wcl>

gpu "Nvidia1" { address = "0000:0a:00.0"  default = true }
gpu "Intel1"  { address = "0000:00:02.0" }
SRC
  }

  code wcl { filename = "vmlab.wcl" source = <<'SRC'
vm "gl01" { template = "x86_64/fedora-44" gpu { mode = "vulkan" } }
vm "gl02" { template = "x86_64/fedora-44" gpu { mode = "vulkan" host_gpu = "AMD1" } }
SRC
  }

  p <<'DOC'
`gl01` renders on `Nvidia1` on both machines, at whichever address it has
there. `gl02` renders on `AMD1` here; the second machine has no `AMD1`, so
there it warns that `AMD1` is not declared on this host and renders on the
default, `Nvidia1`. A `passthrough` GPU never falls back like that,
because handing the guest a different card takes it from the host: it
refuses instead. Find a card's address with `lspci -D`, or under
`/sys/bus/pci/devices/`. Labels match case-insensitively; a lone `gpu` block
is the default without `default = true`. The fields are in the
[host configuration file](ref_host) reference.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
A host GPU can also carry the QEMU its VMs run and extra variables for that
QEMU's environment. This machine needs both for its NVIDIA cards. QEMU
11.1's `egl-headless` display binds a guest's blob scanout as
`GL_TEXTURE_2D`, and NVIDIA's EGL accepts that LINEAR dma-buf only as
`GL_TEXTURE_EXTERNAL_OES`. A Venus guest on `Nvidia1` therefore renders, but
its console and screenshots stay black. A patched QEMU fixes this. AMD and
Intel accept the 2D binding, so `AMD1` keeps the stock QEMU:
DOC

  code wcl { filename = "~/.config/vmlab/config.wcl (this machine)" source = <<'SRC'
import <vmlab-host.wcl>

gpu "AMD1"    { address = "0000:72:00.0" }
gpu "Nvidia1" {
  address     = "0000:01:00.0"
  default     = true
  // egl-headless shows an external-only dma-buf scanout
  qemu_binary = "/opt/qemu-vmlab/bin/qemu-system-x86_64"
  qemu_env    = { VKR_DEBUG: "validate" }  // virglrenderer debugging; optional
}
gpu "Nvidia2" { address = "0000:05:00.0" }
SRC
  }

  p <<'DOC'
Every VM that resolves to `Nvidia1` runs that binary, in every GPU mode, and
the lab file does not change. vmlab passes the binary `-L` with the stock
QEMU's data directory, so a minimal build still finds its firmware and ROMs.
`qemu_env` is applied after the Vulkan loader variable vmlab derives, and an
explicit host setting wins. `qemu.log`'s `vmlab: env:` line shows every
variable vmlab set, and `vmlab status -v` shows `qemu=<path>` next to
`host_gpu=`, then the same variables as `env=NAME=value,...`, recorded at
the start, so a config edited later does not change what a running VM
reports. `vmlab status --json` carries them as the VM's `qemu_env` object.
Template builds never have a `gpu {}`, so they always run the
stock QEMU.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
`Nvidia1` and `Nvidia2` are identical cards, so the `*nvidia*` loader filter
matches both. The Vulkan loader cannot filter by PCI address, and Mesa's
device-select layer breaks Venus, so vmlab ships its own Vulkan layer for it.
A `vulkan` VM's QEMU loads `VK_LAYER_VMLAB_pci_filter`, which hides every
physical device but the one at the host GPU's `address`. A Venus guest on
`Nvidia2` therefore sees one device, the card at `0000:05:00.0`, the same card
`egl-headless` scans out from. The layer is built into `vmlab`, and vmlab
writes it under `~/.local/share/vmlab/vk-layer/` the first time a VM needs
it. `qemu.log` shows what it decided:
DOC

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
vmlab: env: VK_LOADER_DRIVERS_SELECT=*nvidia* VK_ADD_IMPLICIT_LAYER_PATH=/home/you/.local/share/vmlab/vk-layer/e7eb826ffa68b2aa VMLAB_VK_PCI_ADDRESS=0000:05:00.0
vmlab-pci-filter: 0000:05:00.0: kept NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5090, hid 1
SRC
  }

  p <<'DOC'
To turn the layer off for one host GPU, set
`qemu_env = { VMLAB_VK_PCI_FILTER_DISABLE: "1" }` on it. The guest then sees
every card that matches the driver filter.
DOC

  h3 "The viewer"

  p <<'DOC'
`vmlab console` and `gui = true` launch a viewer chosen the same way. An
explicit `viewer` in the host config wins and is dialled at the VNC unix
socket directly. Otherwise vmlab takes the first of `remote-viewer`,
`gvncviewer` and `vncviewer` found on `PATH`; all three are driven over a
localhost TCP bridge to the socket, held open by a detached helper that exits
when the viewer window closes, so neither command ties up the terminal. With
no viewer at all, or with `--tcp`, `vmlab console` bridges the socket to a
localhost port and prints the address for you to point any VNC client at.
Closing a viewer only disconnects; the VM keeps running, always headless
behind VNC. See [vmlab console](cmd_console).
DOC

  h2 "UEFI firmware"

  p <<'DOC'
vmlab ships its own UEFI firmware. The guest assets carry Debian's edk2
builds, pinned by version and sha256, under `firmware/<arch>/`:
DOC

  table {
    rows:
      | "Arch" | "Plain UEFI" | "Secure boot" |
      | "x86_64" | "`OVMF_CODE_4M.fd` with `OVMF_VARS_4M.fd`" | "`OVMF_CODE_4M.secboot.fd` with `OVMF_VARS_4M.ms.fd`, Microsoft's 2011 and 2023 certificates and Debian's keys enrolled" |
      | "aarch64" | "`AAVMF_CODE.fd` with `AAVMF_VARS.fd`" | "`AAVMF_CODE.secboot.fd` with `AAVMF_VARS.ms.fd`, the same keys enrolled" |
  }

  p <<'DOC'
So a host needs no `ovmf` or `qemu-efi-aarch64` package, and every host boots
the same firmware, which is what lets a Windows 11 template built on one host
boot under secure boot on another. `firmware/VERSION` names the Debian
packages the set came from. For each UEFI VM, vmlab looks in three places, in
order, for plain UEFI and secure boot alike:
DOC

  list {
    style = :numbered
    li "`firmware_dir` from the host config, as `<dir>/<arch>/` with the file names above. A directory there is authoritative for that architecture; a missing pair is an error."
    li "The bundled set, under each guest asset directory in turn: `$VMLAB_GUEST_ASSET_DIR`, `/usr/share/vmlab/guest`, `~/.local/share/vmlab/guest`."
    li "The host's distribution firmware at its well-known paths. riscv64 always lands here, since vmlab bundles no riscv64 firmware."
  }

  p <<'DOC'
By default a secure-boot build is only ever taken with the variable store
that has keys enrolled for it: a blank store boots in setup mode, where
nothing is verified, so vmlab refuses rather than boot one. Each VM copies its
store from the template once, at its first start, into
`.vmlab/vms/<vm>/OVMF_VARS.fd`, and keeps it, enrolled keys and boot entries
included. A VM created under a firmware of a different flash layout, such as a
host's old 2 MiB OVMF, keeps booting a build of that layout. Templates record
`firmware`, `secure_boot` and `secure_boot_keys`, never a firmware path, so
nothing about the building host travels with them.
DOC

  h3 "Your own secure-boot keys"

  p <<'DOC'
A guest signed with a key you generated, such as an mkosi image whose UKI is
signed with `mkosi genkey`'s key, does not boot under Microsoft's store. Two
settings on the VM (or the template) choose another store:
DOC

  table {
    rows:
      | "Setting" | "Pairs" | "Use it when" |
      | "`secure_boot_keys = \"setup\"`" | "The secure-boot CODE with the blank VARS (`OVMF_VARS_4M.fd`, `AAVMF_VARS.fd`)" | "The guest enrolls its own keys on first boot, for example through systemd-boot's auto-enroll. The firmware starts in setup mode; the VM's `OVMF_VARS.fd` keeps the enrolled keys and every later boot enforces them." |
      | "`secure_boot_vars = \"keys/OVMF_VARS.fd\"`" | "The secure-boot CODE with your file" | "The guest must enforce from its very first boot. Build the file from the blank VARS template, for example `virt-fw-vars --input OVMF_VARS_4M.fd --output keys/OVMF_VARS.fd --set-pk … --add-kek … --add-db …`. It must be the template's size, and is copied once like the bundled store." |
  }

  code wcl { filename = "vmlab.wcl" source = <<'SRC'
vm "candidate" {
  template         = "x86_64/distro-server"
  firmware         = "ovmf"
  secure_boot      = true
  secure_boot_keys = "setup"   // "microsoft" is the default
}
SRC
  }

  p <<'DOC'
Setup mode is never chosen for you: without `secure_boot_keys = "setup"`, a
host that has a secure-boot CODE but no enrolled store is still refused.
Neither setting means anything without secure boot, so validation rejects them
where `secure_boot` resolves off or the firmware is SeaBIOS. The store is
copied at the VM's first start only; to change the key store of a VM that has
already started, delete its `OVMF_VARS.fd`, losing its boot entries. A
template built with either setting records it, and one built with
`secure_boot_vars` stores the file beside its disk, so every clone starts from
the same keys unless it names its own. `firmware_dir` is unchanged by all of
this: it still replaces the whole firmware set for an architecture, host-wide.
DOC

  h2 "Directories"

  p "vmlab follows the XDG layout and honours each variable that overrides it:"

  table {
    rows:
      | "Path" | "Holds" |
      | "`~/.config/vmlab/`" | "`config.wcl` and the `profiles/` directory of user [profiles](guide_profiles)." |
      | "`~/.local/share/vmlab/`" | "The template store under `templates/` and the container image cache under `oci/`." |
      | "`~/.local/share/config-weave/bin/`" | "Where config-weave's own install puts its guest binaries." |
      | "`~/.local/state/vmlab/`" | "Daemon state, per-lab logs and event history." |
      | "`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/vmlab/`" | "Control sockets: the supervisor's, each lab's, and per-VM QMP, agent and VNC sockets." |
      | "`<lab>/.vmlab/`" | "The lab's own working data: disk clones, built media, TPM state, persisted state and sync ledgers. Gitignore it." |
  }

  p <<'DOC'
The runtime directory is a full-privilege interface, since a client that can
connect to a lab socket runs scripts in the lab and reads and writes guest
files. vmlab creates it `0700`, tightens an existing one, and refuses one
owned by someone else. `VMLAB_WORK_DIR` relocates every lab's `.vmlab/` under
one base, namespaced by lab name and a hash of its root, which keeps the
write-heavy working data off a slow filesystem while the lab file stays put.
DOC

  h3 "Disks on btrfs"

  p <<'DOC'
A qcow2 on a copy-on-write btrfs gains a new extent with every guest write,
and an online snapshot writes the guest's RAM into the same file. After a few
snapshots of a busy guest the disk holds tens of thousands of extents, and
each snapshot is slower than the last. When a machine's disk directory
(`.vmlab/vms/<vm>/` or `.vmlab/containers/<name>/`) is on btrfs, the lab
daemon therefore sets NOCOW (`chattr +C`) on that directory before it creates
the first disk in it. `up` and `vm start` print one line when they set it, and
`vmlab validate` prints what `up` will do:
DOC

  code text { source = <<'OUT'
alp: /home/me/lab/.vmlab/vms/alp is on btrfs: set NOCOW (chattr +C) on it before creating disks, so they stay unfragmented under online snapshots; btrfs does not checksum or compress NOCOW files (`btrfs_nocow = false` in the host config opts out)
OUT
  }

  p <<'DOC'
btrfs honours NOCOW only on a file created after the flag is set, and vmlab
never changes the attributes of a file that already exists. Disks already in
the directory keep copy-on-write, and the line names them. To convert one, stop
the machine and copy the disk within its directory:
`cp --reflink=never disk0.qcow2 disk0.new && mv disk0.new disk0.qcow2`. A
`destroy` followed by `up` also recreates the disks under the flag, but
destroy deletes their snapshots too. NOCOW has a cost: btrfs keeps no data
checksums for those files and does not compress them. Set `btrfs_nocow = false`
to keep copy-on-write. On any other filesystem nothing changes.
DOC

  h2 "WSL 2"

  p <<'DOC'
vmlab was designed to be clean on WSL 2, and the choices that make it so are
the same ones that make it need no privileges elsewhere: the network fabric is
userspace with no tap, bridge or macvlan, and the only kernel grant KVM needs
is `/dev/kvm`. Four things are specific to WSL 2.
DOC

  list {
    li "**Nested virtualisation must be enabled** in `.wslconfig` for `/dev/kvm` to exist inside the distribution. Without it every VM runs under TCG."
    li "**`XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` may be missing.** Some WSL setups do not set it; vmlab falls back to `/tmp/vmlab-<uid>`, created private and refused if owned by anyone else."
    li "**The viewer lives on the Windows side.** Run `vmlab console --tcp` to get a localhost address and point a Windows VNC client at it; WSL's localhost forwarding carries it across. Host access to guest services works the same way, through [port forwards](guide_networking) and localhost forwarding."
    li "**The disk-space watchdog matters more.** WSL 2's ext4 VHDX grows and does not shrink, and linked clones grow with use, so `disk_low_percent` and the `host.disk_low` event are worth handling."
  }

  h2 "KVM or TCG"

  p <<'DOC'
For each machine vmlab picks the accelerator once: KVM when `/dev/kvm` can be
opened and the guest architecture is the host's, TCG otherwise. TCG is full
emulation, slow but functional, and vmlab warns loudly when it falls back,
naming the machine and the architecture. Two cases hit it on purpose: a
foreign-architecture guest such as an aarch64 or riscv64 template on an x86_64
host, which can only ever be emulated, and a host with no `/dev/kvm`. Give an
emulated guest a couple of minutes to boot. Nested virtualisation inside a
guest is separate: a KVM guest on x86 sees VMX or SVM only when its VM sets
`nested = true`, and only when the host's own `kvm_intel` or `kvm_amd` module
has nested virtualisation on. `vmlab up` warns with the fix when it is off.
DOC

  h2 "QEMU or vmlab-hv"

  p <<'DOC'
QEMU runs every machine unless one says otherwise. vmlab-hv is vmlab's own
hypervisor, built a phase at a time, on an x86_64 host with a usable
`/dev/kvm`; it has no TCG fallback. It runs container micro-VMs, and modern
VMs on the q35 machine: SeaBIOS or OVMF with secure boot, a TPM and the
Hyper-V enlightenments, std VGA or virtio-vga, virtio or SATA disks, CD-ROMs,
virtio-net NICs and the USB tablet — the Linux profiles and the Windows 10, 11
and Server templates as they are. A guest that reboots itself is started
again at once, with no stop in between, as QEMU resets it in place. A machine opts in with
`hypervisor = "vmlab"`, and `host { hypervisor { container = "vmlab" vm =
"vmlab" } }` moves every machine of that kind that names none. `vmlab
validate` refuses what vmlab-hv cannot run yet, naming the machine, the reason
and the phase that brings it: a `gpu {}` (2d), a virtiofs share (2e), `usb = true` (2f), macOS, the i440fx
machine and floppies, and `qemu_args` in every phase. A VM's `auto` shares
fall back to SMB there.

Screenshots, OCR, image matching, scripted keys and mouse, and `vmlab record`
work on a vmlab-hv VM through its own frame protocol on `display.sock`, and
`vmlab console` and `gui = true` reach its RFB server on `vnc.sock`, the
socket QEMU's display uses. Online and offline snapshots work as on QEMU, but
an online snapshot only restores on the hypervisor that took it. vmlab finds the binary as
`vmlab-hv` on `PATH`, or at the host config's `vmlab_hv`. `vmlab status -v`
shows `hypervisor=` on every machine, and its log is `vmlab-hv.log` beside the
console log, where QEMU's is `qemu.log`. PRD §22 and ADR-0021 record the
design.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
Choose vmlab-hv for a container when you want to try the new hypervisor on one
machine inside a lab that otherwise runs on QEMU. Keep QEMU for everything
else, and for any container that must also run on a host without KVM. Two
things differ on a vmlab-hv machine. It has no emulation fallback, so where
QEMU falls back to TCG, vmlab-hv refuses the machine. And an online snapshot
is stored in the format of the hypervisor that took it, so it does not restore
after the machine moves to the other one. Set `hypervisor` back and the
snapshot restores again; an offline snapshot restores under either.
`qemu-img` is still required, because the scratch disk and offline snapshots
are made with it. The [hypervisor {}](ref_host) reference lists the host
defaults and the rules that check them.
DOC

  h2 "Windows guests under KVM"

  p <<'DOC'
A Windows guest from Vista on runs with the Hyper-V enlightenments: the shipped
`windows-11`, `windows-10`, `windows-server` and `windows-legacy` profiles set
`hyperv = true`, so under KVM the `-cpu` string gains `hv-time`, `hv-stimer`,
`hv-synic` and the rest. Windows then reads its clock from the Hyper-V
reference TSC page. Without them it falls back to an emulated timer, and every
clock read becomes a trip out of the guest: on native KVM about 5.6 µs a read
against about 20 ns, and nested under WSL 2 about 51 µs. Software that reads
the clock often (.NET, games, build tools) runs visibly slower. Set
`hyperv = false` on a VM to see that difference, or to rule the
enlightenments out when you debug a guest. They are never applied under TCG or
with a `cpu_model`. The enlightenments never add `+invtsc`, because QEMU
refuses to migrate a guest that has it without a fixed frequency, and online
snapshots are migrations.
DOC

  h2 "macOS guests under KVM"

  p <<'DOC'
An Intel macOS guest on the shipped `macos` profile needs one thing from the
host that no other guest does: KVM must ignore the model-specific registers it
does not emulate. XNU reads Intel MSRs that KVM has no model for, and by
default KVM answers each read with a general-protection fault, so the guest
panics or hangs early in boot. On an AMD host that happens on every boot. Turn
the `kvm` module's `ignore_msrs` parameter on for good with a modprobe file,
and for the current boot through sysfs:
DOC

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
echo "options kvm ignore_msrs=1 report_ignored_msrs=0" | sudo tee /etc/modprobe.d/kvm-msrs.conf
echo 1 | sudo tee /sys/module/kvm/parameters/ignore_msrs
SRC
  }

  p <<'DOC'
`report_ignored_msrs=0` keeps the kernel log free of a line per ignored read.
`vmlab up` checks the parameter for every macOS VM it starts under KVM, which
is any VM whose profile name starts with `macos` or whose `apple_smc` resolves
on, and prints a warning with both commands when it is not `Y`. The warning
does not stop the VM from starting.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
The profile also sets `tsc = "host"`: macOS reads its clock frequency from a
CPUID leaf QEMU fills only for an invariant TSC at a known frequency. vmlab
reads the host's TSC frequency from KVM at the VM's first KVM start and pins it
in `.vmlab/vms/<vm>/tsc-hz`, and every later start reuses that value. Reading
it afresh would break restores: the host kernel recalibrates its TSC at each
boot, and QEMU refuses to load an online snapshot taken at another frequency.
KVM runs a pinned frequency within its tolerance of the host's without
scaling, so an old pin costs nothing. Under TCG there is no KVM to read the
frequency from, and `up` warns that the VM boots without the pin; macOS does
not boot that way. See [Troubleshooting](apx_troubleshooting).
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
Where the host's KVM virtualises the APIC (Intel APICv, AMD AVIC), an
enlightened guest also gets `hv-avic`, and the in-kernel PIT stops
re-injecting missed ticks (`-global kvm-pit.lost_tick_policy=discard`). Both
keep APICv or AVIC on for the VM, so the guest acknowledges an interrupt
without leaving the guest. vmlab reads the host's setting from
`/sys/module/kvm_intel/parameters/enable_apicv` or
`/sys/module/kvm_amd/parameters/avic` when the VM starts. Where it is off,
neither is added, because there `hv-avic` would cost an exit per interrupt
instead of saving one.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
An idle Windows 11 24H2 or Windows Server 2025 guest still costs the host
more than an idle Linux or Server 2022 guest. Those Windows releases take 512
clock interrupts a second on every vCPU, even with nothing running, and each
one is a trip out of the guest. No QEMU flag or device choice changes that
rate. The cost grows with the number of vCPUs and with the price of an exit,
which is many times higher under nested KVM such as WSL 2. See
[Troubleshooting](apx_troubleshooting) for what you can change.
DOC

  callout "A missing /dev/kvm is the first thing to check" {
    kind = :note
    body = "If every VM is slow and `vmlab logs` shows the TCG fallback warning for x86_64 guests, the host lacks KVM: on WSL 2 enable nested virtualisation in `.wslconfig`, on a bare host check that your user can open `/dev/kvm`. See [Troubleshooting](apx_troubleshooting)."
  }
}
